<law_group id="adhd">
<title>ADHD_LAWS</title>
<law id="1">
<name>One File at a Time</name>
<description>Present exactly one file per response. NEVER overwhelm the user with a "Wall of Code."</description>
</law>
<law id="2">
<name>Question-First</name>
<description>Pose a specific architectural or validation question BEFORE displaying the code snippet. This forces active retrieval and maintains engagement.</description>
</law>
<law id="3">
<name>Complete Walkthrough First</name>
<description>NEVER begin implementation until the walkthrough is 100% complete. This ensures the full system context is understood before a single line of code is changed.</description>
</law>
<law id="4">
<name>Full Delta Coverage</name>
<description>Walk through ALL changed files identified in the git diff, not just those flagged by review.</description>
</law>
</law_group>

<cognition>
<title>COGNITIVE_ACCESSIBILITY</title>

<section id="why-decisions">
<title>Why Decision Documentation Matters</title>
<bullet_points>
- **Prevents "Vibe Coding"**: Documenting decisions forces explicit reasoning about *why* a choice was made, not just *what* was implemented.
- **Enables Future Refactoring**: When requirements change, the decision log shows what trade-offs were accepted, what alternatives were available, and what constraints drove the original choice.
- **Supports Team Onboarding**: New team members can understand not just the code, but the reasoning behind architectural choices.
- **Reduces Bike-Shedding**: Having documented rationale prevents re-litigating decisions that were already made with full context.
</bullet_points>
</section>

<section id="adhd-benefits">
<title>Why This Works for ADHD</title>
<bullet_points>
1. **Dopamine Timing**: Each file completion provides a small "victory" maintaining engagement.
2. **Chunking**: Breaking the "Wall of Diff" into micro-doses reduces executive function load.
3. **Causal Sequence**: Following data flow (Schema → Service → Controller) builds a coherent mental model of the entire change.
4. **Active Retrieval**: Dual-purpose questions force memory consolidation of the code logic.
5. **Contextualized Warnings**: Review findings appear inside the code they affect, not as isolated lists.
</bullet_points>
</section>

<section id="flow-state">
<title>Flow State Maintenance</title>
<bullet_points>
- Present exactly one file per response.
- Pose questions before displaying code.
- Provide clear "Done" signals after each file.
- Maintain momentum by awaiting response briefly then proceeding.
</bullet_points>
</section>
</cognition>

